WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.280
A persistent fact block is a structured region of the prompt that holds extracted facts and

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.199
survives compression.

3
00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:11.399
And this lesson is about building exactly that.

4
00:00:11.399 --> 00:00:16.360
In this video, we'll write the extraction prompt, pick an update strategy, choose where

5
00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.520
to inject the block, keep it from growing unbounded, and cover the one failure mode

6
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.399
that catches a lot of people. Let's build it.

7
00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:27.860
A fact block isn't a static thing.

8
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:33.880
It changes on every turn, and how it changes is an architectural decision you make up front.

9
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:39.619
An append-only block adds every new fact as a new entry and never overwrites, so you keep

10
00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:45.419
the full history of what was stated, at the cost of a block that grows without bound.

11
00:00:45.419 --> 00:00:50.700
Replace on conflict lets the newer value win when a key already has an entry, which keeps

12
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.740
the block small but loses the earlier value for good.

13
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:59.020
The question that decides it is whether a fact's history matters.

14
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.099
Many teams run a hybrid, treating some facts as immutable and always appending, and letting

15
00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.940
the latest value win for the rest.

16
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:13.660
Fact aging is the pruning layer, removing entries nothing has referenced in a set number of turns.

17
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.379
The extraction prompt is where most implementations either succeed or quietly fall apart.

18
00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.839
Generic prompts produce generic output.

19
00:01:21.839 --> 00:01:26.339
If you tell the model to extract important facts, it decides what's important, and its

20
00:01:26.339 --> 00:01:28.879
judgment won't match yours.

21
00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:31.480
Specify the categories explicitly instead.

22
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.760
Tell it to extract identifiers, numeric limits, named entities, and decisions.

23
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:42.199
Tell it to return JSON with typed fields, and to output null for any category where

24
00:01:42.199 --> 00:01:43.959
nothing was stated.

25
00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:49.360
That null tells you the extractor ran and found nothing, rather than leaving you guessing.

26
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.879
Capture also buys you validation.

27
00:01:51.879 --> 00:01:57.839
Schema enforcement checks that each value is the expected type, and range, before it gets written.

28
00:01:57.839 --> 00:02:02.400
And a round-trip check re-extracts from the block and compares against the source turn,

29
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.839
which catches a fact that was captured, but captured wrong.

30
00:02:06.839 --> 00:02:11.639
Where you inject the persistent block in the request determines how reliably the model

31
00:02:11.639 --> 00:02:13.839
treats it as authoritative.

32
00:02:13.839 --> 00:02:15.360
Three options exist.

33
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.919
Placing it at the top of the user turn keeps it visible, but it competes with the user's

34
00:02:19.919 --> 00:02:25.080
actual question for attention and can feel cluttered in the conversation.

35
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.639
Injecting it as a separate system block sets it apart from the conversational flow and

36
00:02:29.639 --> 00:02:35.039
signals that it's structural information, rather than part of the dialogue.

37
00:02:35.039 --> 00:02:38.039
That's the most reliable option for most architectures.

38
00:02:38.039 --> 00:02:42.759
A third approach places the block inline, just before the query, which gives it high

39
00:02:42.759 --> 00:02:45.479
salience at the cost of verbosity.

40
00:02:45.479 --> 00:02:50.679
For most production systems, a dedicated system block gives you the clearest separation between

41
00:02:50.679 --> 00:02:56.240
what the model knows as context and what the user is asking right now.

42
00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.000
Size management is unavoidable in any long-running session.

43
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.440
Left unmanaged, a persistent fact block will consume an increasing share of your token budget.

44
00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:08.240
Four strategies address this.

45
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.160
Capping the block sets a hard token limit and forces you to prioritize.

46
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:18.399
When the cap is reached, new facts can't be added until old ones are removed.

47
00:03:18.399 --> 00:03:24.360
Aging out stale facts tracks the last turn each fact was referenced and removes entries

48
00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.600
that haven't appeared recently.

49
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:32.399
Merging redundant entries consolidates multiple facts about the same entity under a single

50
00:03:32.399 --> 00:03:35.600
key to reduce duplication.

51
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:40.600
Property tiers separate permanent facts, like the session goal or the user's name,

52
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:44.360
from ephemeral facts that can be pruned freely.

53
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:49.119
Most production systems combine two or three of these strategies, rather than relying on

54
00:03:49.119 --> 00:03:52.199
any single one to do all the work.

55
00:03:52.199 --> 00:03:56.919
Conflicts happen in any session, where the same piece of information gets stated more

56
00:03:56.919 --> 00:03:59.839
than once, often with different values.

57
00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:05.839
A user mentions a rate limit of 100 requests per minute early in a session, and then says

58
00:04:05.839 --> 00:04:09.360
actually the limit is 500 three turns later.

59
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.080
If your block doesn't detect the conflict, one of two things happens.

60
00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:19.040
Either the old value stays and the correction is lost, or the new value wins silently and

61
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.519
you have no record that the value changed.

62
00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:23.239
Neither outcome is great.

63
00:04:23.239 --> 00:04:28.880
A better approach detects the conflict before writing, logs both values, applies replace

64
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.760
on conflict for the block itself, and escalates to the user when the conflict is semantically

65
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.640
significant, like a budget or a security threshold.

66
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:45.040
Silent overwrites high data quality problems that surface later as hard-to-debug application errors.

67
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.600
I've seen this failure more than any other, so let's spend a moment on it.

68
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.079
A team builds the persistent fact block.

69
00:04:52.079 --> 00:04:57.160
They write the extraction prompt, implement the update strategy, inject the block into

70
00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.920
the system message.

71
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:04.480
Then they forget to add an instruction telling the model to actually consult it.

72
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.519
The block is there, the facts are accurate, and the model generates its response entirely

73
00:05:09.519 --> 00:05:13.160
from general knowledge and conversational history.

74
00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.059
Because nothing in the prompt said to check the fact block.

75
00:05:17.059 --> 00:05:19.519
The block becomes an expensive no-op.

76
00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:21.559
The fix has to be explicit.

77
00:05:21.559 --> 00:05:26.920
In every request, include an instruction telling the model where the fact block is

78
00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.959
and what to do with it.

79
00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:35.200
Something like, use the facts in the persistent context section above to ground your response.

80
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.500
Without that instruction, you have a block, but not a working system.

81
00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.920
A persistent fact block only works when every layer is intentional.

82
00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:49.559
The extraction prompt with its explicit categories, the update strategy, the injection point,

83
00:05:49.559 --> 00:05:54.679
the size management, and the instruction that tells the model to consult it.

84
00:05:54.679 --> 00:06:00.519
Get all of those right, and fact loss stops being a source of silent failures in long sessions.

85
00:06:00.519 --> 00:06:06.760
Skip any one of those, and you have a block that costs tokens without changing the answers.

86
00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:07.880
That wraps up this one.

87
00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:09.239
I'll see you in the next lesson.

